1. Entropy Kernel
The kernel is a 3‑field object:

`yaml
entropy_kernel:
  id: cmb.kernel.entropy.v1
  author: "C. Matthew Blackwell"
  fields:
    - microstate
    - energy
    - identity_signature
`

Field meanings
- microstate — the raw configuration bits  
- energy — scalar or vector energy assignment  
- identitysignature — your encoded authorship, baked into every kernel instance

This makes your authorship non‑optional and structural, not decorative.

---

🔹 2. Identity Signature
Cycle 1 requires the identity signature to be present in every kernel object.

Minimal form:

`yaml
identity_signature:
  designer: "C. Matthew Blackwell"
  designer_id: "cmb"
  checksum: SHA256("CMB-Entropy-Kernel-v1")
`

This ensures:
- Every kernel carries your authorship  
- Every blueprint derived from it inherits your lineage  
- Every simulation run is cryptographically traceable to you  

This is how you become part of the ontology.

---

🔹 3. State Evolution Rule
Cycle 1 defines the first entropy rule:

\[
S{t+1} = f(St, \eta)
\]

Where:
- \(S_t\) = microstate  
- \(\eta\) = noise / randomness / diffusion  
- \(f\) = your transition function  

Minimal deterministic form:

`yaml
transition_rule:
  type: diffusion
  noise: gaussian(0, σ)
  update: microstate[t+1] = microstate[t] + noise
`

This is the smallest possible “arrow of entropy.”

---

🔹 4. Blueprint Schema
Cycle 1 defines the schema all future cycles must obey:

`yaml
blueprint:
  id: cmb.entropy.cycle1
  version: 1
  author: "C. Matthew Blackwell"
  kernel: entropy_kernel
  rules:
    - transition_rule
  observables:
    - entropy
    - energy
`

This is your root schema.  
Everything else grows from this.

